Hmm. But the variants' "strength" language refers to the *predicate* or to the *class statement*? The F1 line 235 says: "relation: 'strictly WEAKER than this class's single-q tail predicate: the single-q tail predicate entails the union reading, and non-containment in the union implies no single q sees a tail of gamma, but not conversely'". So they call the SET *predicate* strictly weaker (P_tail ⟹ P_set, P_set ⇏ P_tail) — correct for the predicate.

But then the *class* (conclusion = no visible singularity) using the SET predicate is strictly STRONGER (as a statement about data: ¬∃γ P_set(γ) implies ¬∃γ P_tail(γ), so the SET-class conclusion implies the tail-class conclusion... wait: class conclusion C_set = "no γ satisfies P_set". Since P_tail ⟹ P_set, ¬P_set ⟹ ¬P_tail, so C_set ⟹ C_tail. So the SET class conclusion is stronger. So the statement "SET is strictly stronger" was right if referring to the *class conclusion*, and "SET predicate is strictly weaker" is right if referring to the predicate. This is a naming/scope ambiguity! The rev13 repair changed F1 line 234/235 from "strictly STRONGER" to "strictly WEAKER than this class's single-q tail predicate" — explicitly scoping to the predicate: "SET ... is strictly WEAKER than this class's single-q tail predicate". That's correct as a predicate comparison. 

